<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Grand Duchy of Berg</h1><p class="st-meta">1806 CE – 1813 CE · capital Düsseldorf</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Napoleonic model state on the lower Rhine, ruled first by Joachim Murat and used to spread French law in Germany.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-berg">Duchy of Berg</a> on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, which ceded it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1806. He combined it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a> and other lands and gave it to his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a> as the Grand Duchy of Berg, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> as capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Murat</a> became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1808, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> ruled Berg himself on behalf of his young nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-louis-bonaparte">Napoleon Louis</a>. The grand duchy was organised in French-style <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> and introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>. The industrial region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elberfeld">Elberfeld</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmen">Barmen</a>, with textile and metal trades, suffered from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-system">Continental System</a>, which cut it off from British markets, and conscription led to riots in 1813.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, allied troops took Berg in late 1813. It was placed under provisional administration, and in 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/grand-duchy-of-berg.jpg" alt="Grand Duchy of Berg" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Grand_Duchy_of_Berg_(1806-1808).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1806 CE – 1813 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Düsseldorf</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Joachim Murat, Napoleon I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Großherzogtum Berg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=grand-duchy-of-berg" aria-label="Grand Duchy of Berg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4834 403 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Grand Duchy of Berg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5154 659L5183 683M5154 659L5177 646M5154 659L5154 659M5154 659L5161 628M5154 659L5054 728M5154 659L5199 691M5154 659L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5183 683h0M5177 646h0M5154 659h0M5161 628h0M5054 728h0M5199 691h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5154 659h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5154 659h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Düsseldorf. <a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=grand-duchy-of-berg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
